INT. CORPORATE EXECUTIVE SUITE - BOARDROOM - DAY

A polished glass table. Leather chairs. A panoramic window onto a gray skyline. In the corner, a speakerphone the size of a pie.

DIRECTOR PENROSE (60s, silver suit, rimless glasses) sits at the head of the table, hands steepled. WALDO (40s, VP of Synergy, pastel tie) shuffles papers to his right. SADIE (20s, intern, a cheap blazer) stands behind him holding a tray of lukewarm coffees. GREER (50s, CFO, pearl necklace) sits to Penrose's left, perfectly still.

A sleek black speakerphone on the table glows green. A woman's voice, crisp and flat, emerges.

TAMARA (V.O.)
(speakerphone)
Mr. Penrose, I have the board on line two.

PENROSE
Hold them, Tamara.

TAMARA (V.O.)
(speakerphone)
They said it's urgent.

PENROSE
Everything is urgent. Hold them.

TAMARA (V.O.)
(speakerphone)
Yes, sir.

Waldo smiles at Greer. Greer does not smile back. Sadie slides a coffee in front of Penrose. He doesn't touch it.

WALDO
Director, I believe we're all aligned on the quarterly...

PENROSE
Waldo.

WALDO
Yes?

PENROSE
Are the numbers real?

WALDO
They're, uh... aspirational.

PENROSE
Greer?

GREER
They're numbers.

Penrose lifts the coffee. Sniffs. Sets it back down. Sadie stands rigid. Beads of sweat form at Waldo's hairline.

TAMARA (V.O.)
(speakerphone)
Mr. Penrose, line two is insisting.

PENROSE
Put them through.

A click. A burst of voices. The speakerphone lights red. Greer closes her folder. Waldo tugs at his pastel tie.

PENROSE (CONT'D)
Good morning, gentlemen.

Penrose rises, walks to the window, hands behind his back. He stares out at the skyline. Behind him, Waldo whispers something to Sadie. Sadie nods too fast.

WALDO
(low, to Sadie)
Get me a glass of water.

SADIE
There's coffee.

WALDO
Water. Now.

She hurries out. The door closes. The speakerphone drones on, a muffled crowd of men reading numbers aloud.

GREER
Director.

PENROSE
Yes, Greer.

GREER
Whatever you intend to do, do it before the lunch hour.

Penrose turns from the window. For the first time, he smiles.

PENROSE
Waldo. Come here.

Waldo stands. His knees pop. He walks around the table, unsteady, to the window. Penrose puts a hand on his shoulder and points down at the street, thirty floors below.

PENROSE (CONT'D)
Look at them.

WALDO
At... the pedestrians?

PENROSE
That is what real numbers look like.

Waldo stares. Greer folds her hands. On the speakerphone, someone coughs, and the voices stop. The whole room hangs. Penrose lifts his hand from Waldo's shoulder, and presses a button on the wall. The glass table slowly begins to tilt.
